WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.402
A payment authorization takes 200 ms.

2
00:00:05.402 --> 00:00:09.711
It calls the fraud service, the card issuer and the ledger.

3
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:12.499
Only 5 ms of it runs on a CPU.

4
00:00:12.499 --> 00:00:15.493
The rest is waiting on the network.

5
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.113
Yet the request holds one thread for all 200 ms.

6
00:00:19.113 --> 00:00:22.881
While it waits, no other request can use that thread.

7
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.407
Here is a pool of 200 threads.

8
00:00:26.407 --> 00:00:31.091
Each square fills up as its request runs, then takes the next request.

9
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:36.661
At 800 requests a second, 160 are busy, and 40 sit idle.

10
00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:40.528
Now traffic rises to 1,500 a second.

11
00:00:40.528 --> 00:00:41.851
What happens?

12
00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:47.743
The idle threads fill at once, and new requests start to queue.

13
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.961
Each thread finishes five requests a second, so 200 threads finish 1,000 a second, and not one more.

14
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.647
The other 500 a second pile up.

15
00:00:57.647 --> 00:01:00.568
After ten seconds, 5,000 are waiting.

16
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:01.617
Freeze it.

17
00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:04.723
Only about 5 threads are running code.

18
00:01:04.723 --> 00:01:07.583
The other 195 are blocked, waiting.

19
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.391
You run out of threads long before you run out of CPU.

20
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:16.424
Now say the card issuer slows down to 1.2 seconds a call.

21
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:23.393
The same 200 threads now finish only 156 a second, and your code did not change.

22
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.284
Why not run 2,000 threads?

23
00:01:27.284 --> 00:01:31.920
Each one is a platform thread: one Java thread is one OS thread.

24
00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:34.902
Each reserves a 1 MB stack by default.

25
00:01:34.902 --> 00:01:41.896
Starting one is a system call, and switching between thousands costs kernel time and CPU caches.

26
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:49.861
So a JVM is fine with hundreds, survives a few thousand, and fights the OS at tens of thousands.

27
00:01:49.861 --> 00:01:51.773
A million is out of reach.

28
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.040
This is the thread-per-request wall.

29
00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:00.436
In Java and Kotlin it looks harmless: a fixed pool, and each task keeps its thread.

30
00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:01.907
The rule.

31
00:02:01.907 --> 00:02:08.811
A blocked request still holds its thread, so threads divided by time per request is your ceiling.

32
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:10.054
Check yourself.

33
00:02:10.054 --> 00:02:13.454
200 threads, and each request now takes 2 seconds.

34
00:02:13.454 --> 00:02:16.243
How many can the service finish a second?

35
00:02:18.967 --> 00:02:19.413
100.

36
00:02:19.413 --> 00:02:23.097
200 threads divided by 2 seconds.
